& [Situation]
  + Cantor already has signed SOP package admission deterministic procedure-candidate compilation process IR verification runtime forms ordinary llama.cpp tool loops and a newly completed lexical association sidecar
  + Speak Into Order already distinguishes self-description self-ordering self-hosting and self-revision while requiring external trust and approval
  + the new dictation converges these pieces on one SOP compiler front end with three distinct output targets

& [Justification]
  + a common typed semantic front end can preserve meaning source constraints faculty accounts and backend selection before toolchain-specific work begins
  + separate backends are necessary because an attention frame an inference-host extension and a native binary have different execution semantics risks verification methods and rollback identities
  + a small admitted seed can name trust roots identities dependencies discovery rules and permitted compiler entry points without embedding the full mature system
  + staged seed expansion can make Cantor self-ordering and eventually self-hosting while external recognition prevents self-description from becoming self-granted authority

& [Delineation]
  + SOPSource is preserved human-authored meaning and never executable merely because it parses
  + CanonicalSOP is SJS-processed typed declaration and still grants no backend execution
  + SemanticCompilerFrontEnd resolves exact identities contracts dependencies capabilities and one requested backend into typed SOP IR
  + AttentionProcedureBackend produces model-visible procedure frames and host-executed transition contracts
  + InferenceHostBackend produces external tool adapters first and version-pinned llama.cpp internal extensions only after a measured interface-limitation receipt
  + NativeArtifactBackend produces build plans source manifests external binary service or HDL candidates through identified conventional toolchains
  + Admission verifies candidate bytes receipts scopes and external recognition before any artifact becomes a trusted installed generation

& [SJS]
  + Situation: the project has multiple compiler-like mechanisms but no one architecture stating how a signed seed grows through distinct executable targets
  + Justification: without explicit strata the word compile could launder prose into execution or collapse attention contracts host integrations and native artifacts into one risk class
  + Solution: specify one source-preserving semantic front end three separately gated backends a non-self-authorizing admission boundary and a staged self-assembly ledger

& [DualHemisphere]
  + divergent projection considers source generation build orchestration plugin ABI tool schema model procedure FPGA and self-hosting interpretations
  + convergent grounding retains only three backend classes beneath one semantic front end because those classes have distinct execution and proof behavior

& [FacultyReview]
  + Honesty requires source-to-IR correspondence explicit nonclaims and no equivalence between semantic validity compilation success and external truth
  + Security separates backend capabilities trust roots signing identities toolchains sandboxes installations and rollback generations
  + Weaver maps one typed IR graph to several possible backend plans without merging their permissions
  + Planner orders preservation SJS typing backend selection build verification admission installation and observation
  + Refiner keeps the seed minimal and prefers an external llama.cpp tool seam before internal modification
  + Scribe records exact source IR build artifact receipt signature admission and generation identities
  + Observer requires per-backend tests reproducibility mutation refusal rollback and operational evidence before maturity advances

& [Disposition]
  + [architecture] ready_for_canonical_formation
  + [implementation] not_activated
  + [next_dependency] complete proof-bearing lexical query then define a provider-free seed planning fixture before any artifact-emitting backend
  + [still_locked] arbitrary SOP execution autonomous self-signing automatic installation llama.cpp modification native compilation deployment FPGA emission remote mutation and authority growth
